{"id":"rental-assistance-gap","title":"Emergency rent aid: what San Francisco spent against what it says reached households","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"Of the federal emergency rent money the City spent, how much do its own reported household numbers account for?","finding":"San Francisco told the Treasury it spent $25.4 million and helped 2,211 households with an average of $7,021 each, which comes to $15.5 million, or 61% of the spending. The other $9.9 million is not explained in the federal file. Among the 35 other California cities and counties in the same report the figure is 90% at the median and never below 81% (Stanislaus County); San Francisco is the lowest of 36.","how":"From the Treasury's final report on the first round of Emergency Rental Assistance (2021 to 2022): each grantee's households assisted times its average assistance per household, divided by its total spending. The programme allowed up to 10% for administration and housing-stability services, so about 90% is what a grantee that reported consistently should show. The City's monthly reports to the Treasury tell the same story: $13.9 million paid to households by June 2022, which the Treasury's own measure put at 59% of the 90% of the grant meant for households.","limits":"The federal file does not split aid from administration, so the gap is worked out, not reported. A reporting slip would produce the same picture: an average worked out per payment and not per household, or households under-counted in the final report, would produce the same picture. The Treasury accepted the report and no audit finding on it is on file. The State ran its own, separate programme for San Francisco tenants.","columns":[{"k":"grantee","t":"Grantee","type":"text"},{"k":"kind","t":"Type","type":"text"},{"k":"allocation","t":"Federal grant","type":"usd"},{"k":"spent","t":"Total spent","type":"usd"},{"k":"households","t":"Households helped","type":"int"},{"k":"average","t":"Average aid reported","type":"usd"},{"k"